        <div id="white-bg-section">
            <div id="section-icon" style="background:url(../images/brainstorm-icon.png) no-repeat 50% 0px;"></div>
            <div id="section-title">Conceptual</div>
            <div id="section-text-middle" style="width:600px;text-align:center;">After a thorough research process that provides us with valuable information data for our project, we can begin to create tangible realizations of our discoveries.</div>
            
            <!--PERSONAS-->
            <div style="margin:0 auto;height:150px;width:880px;border-top:2px solid black;border-bottom:1px solid black;padding:30px 0px 30px 0px;">
                <div id="section-subtitle-large" style="float:left;width:305px;padding:30px 0px 30px 0px;">Personas<br />&amp; profiles</div>
                <div id="section-text" style="float:right;width:575px;padding:30px 0px 30px 0px;">
                	The information gathered from the park visit interviews and extensive analysis helped define these elements. In addition to people, we even developed profiles for the park itself, park visitor documents, and technology.<br /><br />
					<i>*These are hypothetical since we haven't actually visited the park yet</i>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div id="section-text" style="height:730px;border-bottom:1px solid black;padding:0px 0px 30px 0px;font-size:14px;text-align:left;">
                    <div style="display:inline;padding:60px 0px 0px 0px;width:175px;float:left;">
                    	<div style="width:150px;height:150px;border-radius:50%;border:5px solid #dedede;background:url(images/persona-icons.png) no-repeat 0px 4px;"></div>
                        <div style="height:30px;"></div>
                        <font style="display:block;font-weight:bold;color:black;font-size:18px;">Parents</font><br />
                        <b>Behaviors</b><br />We discovered that most parents tend to designate meeting spots for their party when they are at the park. In the even that they lose a child, they first look in their close surroundings, then the meeting spot, and eventually staff if they still can’t find their child.<br /><br />
                        <b>Possessions</b>
                        <ul class="no-indent">
                            <li>Snacks</li>
                            <li>Mobile phone</li>
                            <li>Personal wallet or handbag</li>
                        </ul>
					</div>
                    <div style="display:inline;margin:0px 0px 0px 60px;padding:60px 0px 0px 0px;width:175px;float:left;">
                    	<div style="width:150px;height:150px;border-radius:50%;border:5px solid #dedede;background:url(images/persona-icons.png) no-repeat -218px 4px;"></div>
                        <div style="height:30px;"></div>
                        <font style="display:block;font-weight:bold;color:black;font-size:18px;">Child</font><br />
                        <b>Behaviors</b><br />Most children were educated to go to a meeting spot if they get lost. If they had trouble finding the meeting spot, they would generally turn to their favorite amusement park character to help them (i.e. Mickey Mouse). Most were afraid to talk to strangers.<br /><br />
                        <b>Possessions</b>
                        <ul class="no-indent">
                            <li>Candy</li>
                        </ul>
					</div>
                    <div style="display:inline;margin:0px 0px 0px 60px;padding:60px 0px 0px 0px;width:175px;float:left;">
                    	<div style="width:150px;height:150px;border-radius:50%;border:5px solid #dedede;background:url(images/persona-icons.png) no-repeat -436px 4px;"></div>
                        <div style="height:30px;"></div>
                        <font style="display:block;font-weight:bold;color:black;font-size:18px;">Info &amp; security staff</font><br />
                        <b>Behaviors</b><br />In the event of a crisis such as a lost child, staff members first tend to report the situation to the head office over their radio. They then assess and address the situation.<br /><br />
                        <b>Possessions</b>
                        <ul class="no-indent">
                            <li>Distinct uniform and identification</li>
                            <li>Radio</li>
                            <li>Mobile phone</li>
                            <li>Skeleton keys to park</li>
                        </ul>
					</div>
                    <div style="display:inline;margin:0px 0px 0px 60px;padding:60px 0px 0px 0px;width:175px;float:left;">
                    	<div style="width:150px;height:150px;border-radius:50%;border:5px solid #dedede;background:url(images/persona-icons.png) no-repeat -658px 4px;"></div>
                        <div style="height:30px;"></div>
                        <font style="display:block;font-weight:bold;color:black;font-size:18px;">Service staff</font><br />
                        <b>Behaviors</b><br />This is separate from the first group of staff and is made up of janitorial, maintenance, and food service members. The difference between them and the first staff group is that they aren’t equipped with as many tools in case of an emergency. In the event of an emergency, they tend to call the head office on their mobile phone or alert nearby security personnel.<br /><br />
                        <b>Possessions</b>
                        <ul class="no-indent">
                            <li>Mobile phone</li>
                            <li>Service-related room keys</li>
                        </ul>
					</div>
            </div>
            <div id="section-text" style="height:660px;border-bottom:2px solid black;padding:0px 0px 30px 0px;font-size:14px;text-align:left;">
                    <div style="display:inline;padding:60px 0px 0px 0px;width:175px;float:left;">
                    	<div style="width:150px;height:150px;border-radius:50%;border:5px solid #dedede;background:url(images/persona-icons.png) no-repeat -875px 4px;"></div>
                        <div style="height:30px;"></div>
                        <font style="display:block;font-weight:bold;color:black;font-size:18px;">Other visitors</font><br />
                        <b>Behaviors</b><br />In emergency situations such as encountering a lost child, visitors mentioned that they generally try to locate a nearby staff member to assist or resolve the situation.
<br /><br />
                        <b>Possessions</b>
                        <ul class="no-indent">
                            <li>Mobile phone</li>
                            <li>Snacks</li>
                            <li>Personal wallet or handbag</li>
                        </ul>
					</div>
                    <div style="display:inline;margin:0px 0px 0px 60px;padding:60px 0px 0px 0px;width:175px;float:left;">
                    	<div style="width:150px;height:150px;border-radius:50%;border:5px solid #dedede;background:url(images/persona-icons.png) no-repeat -1097px 4px;"></div>
                        <div style="height:30px;"></div>
                        <font style="display:block;font-weight:bold;color:black;font-size:18px;">Park environment</font><br />
                        <b>Defined layout</b><br />By starting with the existing park map, we would enhance it to create one that is inclusive of all useful information in the park.<br /><br />
                        <b>Includes</b>
                        <ul class="no-indent">
                            <li>Signage</li>
                            <li>Potential meeting spots</li>
                            <li>Parking sections</li>
                            <li>etc.</li>
                        </ul>
					</div>
                    <div style="display:inline;margin:0px 0px 0px 60px;padding:60px 0px 0px 0px;width:175px;float:left;">
                    	<div style="width:150px;height:150px;border-radius:50%;border:5px solid #dedede;background:url(images/persona-icons.png) no-repeat -1314px 4px;"></div>
                        <div style="height:30px;"></div>
                        <font style="display:block;font-weight:bold;color:black;font-size:18px;">Park documents</font><br />
                        <b>Visitor materials</b><br />By realizing all documents that a visitor may receive before and during their park visit, we can later determine if we can redesign the documents to our benefit.<br /><br />
                        <b>Includes</b>
                        <ul class="no-indent">
                            <li>Park brochure information</li>
                            <li>Admission tickets</li>
                            <li>Park map</li>
                            <li>Receipts</li>
                            <li>Email receipts</li>
                        </ul>
					</div>
                    <div style="display:inline;margin:0px 0px 0px 60px;padding:60px 0px 0px 0px;width:175px;float:left;">
                    	<div style="width:150px;height:150px;border-radius:50%;border:5px solid #dedede;background:url(images/persona-icons.png) no-repeat -1537px 4px;"></div>
                        <div style="height:30px;"></div>
                        <font style="display:block;font-weight:bold;color:black;font-size:18px;">Technology</font><br />
                        <b>Current tech</b><br />This list defines all of the technology that exists at the park - both those that exist with visitors and staff along with those that are stationary.<br /><br />
                        <b>Includes</b>
                        <ul class="no-indent">
                            <li>Mobile phones</li>
                            <li>Radios</li>
                            <li>Speakerphones</li>
                            <li>Sirens</li>
                            <li>Televisions</li>
                            <li>Cameras</li>
                            <li>Golf karts</li>
                        </ul>
					</div>
            </div>
            <!--END PERSONAS-->
            
            <div style="clear:both;height:60px;"></div>
            
            <div id="section-subtitle-large" style="margin:0 auto;width:880px;padding:30px 0px 15px 0px;text-align:center;">Unique journey maps</div>
            <div id="section-text-middle" style="border:0px solid red;padding:0px 0px 60px 0px;width:600px;font-weight:normal;text-align:justify;">Accompanying each profile that make up the listed personas are individual journeys maps, also called customer journey maps, that  were outlined for parents, children, staff members, and other park visitors. These helped realize a typical day at the park and define all the touch points of these individuals. By understanding these touch points, it enables us to examine ways we can leverage each of them to contribute to the solution (i.e. - using receipts to drive users to download the app). Below is a user flow for a parent and child which is  validated from our enacted park experience research and interviews that we conducted.</div>
			<div style="margin:0 auto;width:696px;height:409px;background:url(images/laptop-1.png) no-repeat 0px 0px;"></div>
			<div id="section-text-middle" style="border:0px solid red;padding:15px 0px 60px 0px;width:600px;font-weight:normal;text-align:center;"><a style="font-size:14px;" href="pdf/user-journey-map.pdf" target="_blank" class="link">Click here to download and preview this customer journey map in detail</a></div>
            <div id="section-text-middle" style="border:0px solid red;padding:0px 0px 60px 0px;width:600px;font-weight:normal;text-align:justify;">Its important to identify all possible child behaviors and actions if he separates from his parents so we can provide the parent(s) the proper tools and information within the app to reunite them quickly. As you can see, all scenarios need to be defined, even the worst cases where a child may be abducted. Proper planning in advance helps avoid the most catastrophic results.</div>                    
        	
            <div style="clear:both;height:0px;"></div>
            
            <div id="section-subtitle" style="margin:auto;width:880px;padding:60px 0px 60px 0px;border-top:1px solid black;text-align:center;">After realizing the aforementioned personas and profiles, it actually helped us discover that these are all of the resources that can be optimized to communicate and collaborate with one another in the event of a lost child.</div>
        	
            <div style="clear:both;height:0px;"></div>
            
            <div style="margin:0 auto;width:880px;height:873px;background:url(images/diagram.png) no-repeat 0px 0px;">
            	<div id="section-subtitle" style="margin:auto;width:400px;padding:350px 0px 60px 0px;border-top:0px solid black;text-align:justify;">How do we develop an improved system that enables all of these elements to work together more efficiently and collaboratively so that in the event of a child getting separated from his/her parents, they can be reunited quickly?</div>
            </div>
            
            <div style="clear:both;height:120px;"></div>
            
            <!--PERSONAS-->
            <div style="margin:0 auto;height:150px;width:880px;border-top:2px solid black;border-bottom:1px solid black;padding:30px 0px 30px 0px;">
                <div id="section-subtitle-large" style="float:left;width:470px;padding:30px 0px 30px 0px;">Applying the<br />personas &amp; profiles</div>
                <div id="section-text" style="float:right;width:410px;padding:30px 0px 30px 0px;">With these defined reference points, they helped provide an understanding to develop ways they can all contribute in the event of a lost child or what potential obstacles inhibit them from being an effective part of the solution.</div>
            </div>
            
            <div style="clear:both;height:0px;"></div>
            
            <div id="section-text" style="height:700px;border-bottom:1px solid black;padding:0px 0px 30px 0px;font-size:14px;text-align:left;">
                    <div style="display:inline;padding:60px 0px 0px 0px;width:175px;float:left;">
                    	<div style="width:150px;height:150px;border-radius:50%;border:5px solid #dedede;background:url(images/persona-icons.png) no-repeat 0px 4px;"></div>
                        <div style="height:30px;"></div>
                        <font style="display:block;font-weight:bold;color:black;font-size:18px;">Parents</font><br />
                        Since parents tend to designate meeting spots in the park themselves, wouldn’t it be ideal if parks actually defined highly visible meeting spots?<br />Also, how can we help parents locate a staff member quickly through the app if they want to seek one for assistance?
					</div>
                    <div style="display:inline;margin:0px 0px 0px 60px;padding:60px 0px 0px 0px;width:175px;float:left;">
                    	<div style="width:150px;height:150px;border-radius:50%;border:5px solid #dedede;background:url(images/persona-icons.png) no-repeat -218px 4px;"></div>
                        <div style="height:30px;"></div>
                        <font style="display:block;font-weight:bold;color:black;font-size:18px;">Child</font><br />
                        Since many children tend to locate a meeting spot designated by their parents if they get lost, wouldn’t it be ideal if parks created visibile meeting spots and helped provide guidance to get to these locations?
					</div>
                    <div style="display:inline;margin:0px 0px 0px 60px;padding:60px 0px 0px 0px;width:175px;float:left;">
                    	<div style="width:150px;height:150px;border-radius:50%;border:5px solid #dedede;background:url(images/persona-icons.png) no-repeat -436px 4px;"></div>
                        <div style="height:30px;"></div>
                        <font style="display:block;font-weight:bold;color:black;font-size:18px;">Info &amp; security staff</font><br />
                        This team works hard to ensure visitors have the best experience at the park and address any problems that may arise. In addition to children who get separated from their parents, they have a number of other tasks that include maintenance requests, assisting visitors with disabilities, amongst others. In fact, lost children actually make up only 5% of their tasks. Can the app we build be designed to assist this team in resolving any issues they may encounter?
					</div>
                    <div style="display:inline;margin:0px 0px 0px 60px;padding:60px 0px 0px 0px;width:175px;float:left;">
                    	<div style="width:150px;height:150px;border-radius:50%;border:5px solid #dedede;background:url(images/persona-icons.png) no-repeat -658px 4px;"></div>
                        <div style="height:30px;"></div>
                        <font style="display:block;font-weight:bold;color:black;font-size:18px;">Service staff</font><br />
                        Since service staff aren’t equipped with the extra tools of their counterparts like a radio, they can only rely on their mobile phone to call the head office or look in the park for a nearby staff member. At times this can be dificult to track a nearby staff colleague. Can the app be designed to help improve the way they can assist in dire situations?
					</div>
            </div>
            <div id="section-text" style="height:600px;border-bottom:2px solid black;padding:0px 0px 30px 0px;font-size:14px;text-align:left;">
                    <div style="display:inline;padding:60px 0px 0px 0px;width:175px;float:left;">
                    	<div style="width:150px;height:150px;border-radius:50%;border:5px solid #dedede;background:url(images/persona-icons.png) no-repeat -875px 4px;"></div>
                        <div style="height:30px;"></div>
                        <font style="display:block;font-weight:bold;color:black;font-size:18px;">Other visitors</font><br />
                        Most visitors who aren’t capable of resolving a problem will seek out a nearby staff member for help. Can we build the app to help them find a staff member quicker? Also, why would they have the app if they don’t anticipate losing a child? Can the app be designed to have other incentives that would encourage everyone to download it?
					</div>
                    <div style="display:inline;margin:0px 0px 0px 60px;padding:60px 0px 0px 0px;width:175px;float:left;">
                    	<div style="width:150px;height:150px;border-radius:50%;border:5px solid #dedede;background:url(images/persona-icons.png) no-repeat -1097px 4px;"></div>
                        <div style="height:30px;"></div>
                        <font style="display:block;font-weight:bold;color:black;font-size:18px;">Park environment</font><br />
                        With the current layout and makeup of the park, what can be enhanced to help cater to crisis situations such as a lost child? Since people tend to create meeting spots, can the park help define those areas visibly? Can park signage be utilized to drive people towards meeting spots or potentially download the app?
					</div>
                    <div style="display:inline;margin:0px 0px 0px 60px;padding:60px 0px 0px 0px;width:175px;float:left;">
                    	<div style="width:150px;height:150px;border-radius:50%;border:5px solid #dedede;background:url(images/persona-icons.png) no-repeat -1314px 4px;"></div>
                        <div style="height:30px;"></div>
                        <font style="display:block;font-weight:bold;color:black;font-size:18px;">Park documents</font><br />
                        The documents provided to park visitors are a park information brochure, admission tickets, park map, paper receipts, and/or email receipts. What useful information can these materials reveal about the park app or meeting spots?
					</div>
                    <div style="display:inline;margin:0px 0px 0px 60px;padding:60px 0px 0px 0px;width:175px;float:left;">
                    	<div style="width:150px;height:150px;border-radius:50%;border:5px solid #dedede;background:url(images/persona-icons.png) no-repeat -1537px 4px;"></div>
                        <div style="height:30px;"></div>
                        <font style="display:block;font-weight:bold;color:black;font-size:18px;">Technology</font><br />
                        Since everyone in the park except children is equipped with a mobile phone, the app can be a significant contributor to helping address any problems if designed properly. What other technology at the park can be used to work in tandem with the app and people? Is there any new technology that can be introduced to add another assistance mechanism?
					</div>
            </div>
            <!--END PERSONAS-->
            
            <div style="clear:both;height:60px;"></div>
            
            <div id="section-subtitle" style="margin:auto;width:880px;padding:0px 0px 60px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid black;text-align:center;">These personas and profiles answer the questions above and help reveal behaviors and properties that would be carefully taken into consideration when developing design solutions.</div>
            
            <div style="margin:0 auto;height:220px;width:880px;border-top:0px solid black;border-bottom:0px solid black;padding:30px 0px 30px 0px;">
                <div id="section-subtitle-large" style="margin:0 auto;width:470px;padding:60px 0px 30px 0px;text-align:center;">Idea generation</div>
                <div id="section-text" style="margin:0 auto;width:550px;padding:0px 0px 30px 0px;">After all of the research that has been conducted and with these personas and profiles defined, we need to begin documenting all of the ideas that have been slowly building up in our minds. No idea is a bad idea at this point - its important to throw everything out there so that we have a densely rich field to examine. Below are some of the ideas that were created.</div>
            </div>
            
            <div style="clear:both;height:60px;"></div>
            
            <div style="margin:0 auto;width:880px;height:820px;border-bottom:1px solid black;background:url(images/brainstorm-clouds.png) no-repeat 0px 0px;">
            	<div id="section-text" style="margin:auto;width:550px;padding:690px 0px 60px 0px;border-top:0px solid black;text-align:justify;font-size:14px;">All of these ideas were vetted by considering each of their pros and cons and also weighing them against our initial outline of goals and constraints. Its important to document all of these ideas, even those that aren't used, because they can all be explored and possibly implemented in a future update.</div>
            </div>
            
            <div style="clear:both;height:30px;"></div>
            
            <div style="margin:0 auto;height:190px;width:880px;border-top:0px solid black;border-bottom:0px solid black;padding:30px 0px 30px 0px;">
                <div id="section-subtitle-large" style="margin:0 auto;width:470px;padding:0px 0px 30px 0px;text-align:center;">Potential solution</div>
                <div id="section-text" style="margin:0 auto;width:550px;padding:0px 0px 0px 0px;">After exploring the vast amount of ideas submitted by the team, it's important to consolidate the best ideas and eventually converge them into one build that we believe is optimal. Below are key features of that solution.</div>
            </div>
            
            <div style="clear:both;height:0px;"></div>
            
            <div id="section-subtitle" style="margin:0 auto;padding:15px 0px 15px 0px;width:880px;border-top:2px solid black;border-bottom:1px solid black;">Key features</div>
        	<div id="section-text" style="height:600px;border-bottom:1px solid black;padding:30px 0px 30px 0px;font-size:14px;">
            	<div style="display:inline;width:250px;float:left;">
                	<div style="width:250px;height:150px;background:url(images/solution-icons.png) no-repeat 60px 15px;"></div>
                    <div style="height:30px;"></div>
                	<font style="display:block;font-weight:bold;color:black;"><br />Hook users in with incentive</font>Users may not download an app if it only helps find lost children - they'll simply make a stronger effort to make sure that doesn't happen. They also wouldn't take the time to download the app if they did lose their child - they will just quickly look for a staff member to help. However, if the app serves a greater purpose like enabling Fastpass booking capability or displaying line wait times for rides, there are more reasons to have it. Any desired information or request within the park can be delivered in the app - including seeking staff help if one should lose their child.
                </div>
                <div style="margin:0px 0px 0px 65px;display:inline;width:250px;float:left;">
                	<div style="width:250px;height:150px;background:url(images/solution-icons.png) no-repeat -215px 4px;"></div>
                    <div style="height:30px;"></div>
                	<font style="display:block;font-weight:bold;color:black;"><br />Interactive map</font>The map displays all the minute details of the park such as designated meeting points, restrooms, restaurants, park zones and attractions, etc. In addition, it also enables users to see nearby staff and immediately request one for assistance if it is needed - a handy feature if there is no park staff in sight.
                </div>
                <div style="margin:0px 0px 0px 65px;display:inline;width:250px;float:left;">
					<div style="width:250px;height:170px;background:url(images/solution-icons.png) no-repeat -500px 14px;"></div>
                    <div style="height:10px;"></div>                	
                    <font style="display:block;font-weight:bold;color:black;"><br />Marked meeting spots & pathways</font>Since people naturally tend to designate their own meeting spots in case they get lost - why not have the park define these locations? They can do this by implementing distinct signs such as those I've seen at <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meeting_point" target="_blank" class="link" style="font-size:14px;">Singapore airport or similar other publicly defined meeting points</a>. Pathways could be marked by a center line leading up to these meeting points. These pathway guide lines not only lead people up to a meeting point but also provide a specific area to search within the span of the entire pathway if you happen to lose someone at the park. The idea that sparked this concept was the <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Freedom_Trail" target="_blank" class="link" style="font-size:14px;">Freedom Trail</a> in Boston, Massachusetts.
                </div>
            </div>
            <div id="section-text" style="height:680px;clear:both;border-bottom:2px solid black;padding:30px 0px 30px 0px;font-size:14px;">
            	<div style="display:inline;width:250px;float:left;">
                	<div style="width:250px;height:150px;background:url(images/solution-icons.png) no-repeat -770px 4px;"></div>
                    <div style="height:30px;"></div>
                    <font style="display:block;font-weight:bold;color:black;">ID wristband tickets</font>Current admission tickets aren't utilized in any way after entry so why not make the ticket a wristband that outwardly displays legitimate patrons? All members of a party would be provided these wristbands that share one unique ID number printed on them. This ID number would be tied to only the purchaser's name and phone number that is provided during ticket purchase (simpler than providing multiple contacts which would lead to longer lines and wait times at the entrance). The visible ID number would conceal the personal information - only staff would be able to look up the associated name and phone number through the app. They remain economical since they would still be printed on paper yet the ID number amplifies its purpose.
                </div>
                <div style="margin:0px 0px 0px 65px;display:inline;width:250px;float:left;">
                	<div style="width:250px;height:150px;background:url(images/solution-icons.png) no-repeat -1020px 4px;"></div>
                    <div style="height:30px;"></div>
                	<font style="display:block;font-weight:bold;color:black;">Enhanced reporting</font>This feature would enable staff to be able to file any reports and incidents on their own rather than having to radio it into the office. Since lost child cases only make up a minute fraction of daily requests, why not design the app to be more generalized and let the staff outline the specifics? From maintenance requests to lost child cases, park staff can be empowered to manage and maintain case requests on their own.
                </div>
                <div style="margin:0px 0px 0px 65px;display:inline;width:250px;float:left;">
					<div style="width:250px;height:150px;background:url(images/solution-icons.png) no-repeat -1280px 4px;"></div>
                    <div style="height:30px;"></div>                
                	<font style="display:block;font-weight:bold;color:black;">Swift &amp; vast-reaching alert system</font>In the event that a child has been missing for an extended period of time, all staff can be alerted through the app and if child abduction is of potential concern, these alerts can even extend to notifying everyone at the park. All visitors could be made aware of the situation and they can serve as extra eyes in the search effort.
                </div>
            </div>
            
            <div id="section-subtitle" style="margin:auto;width:880px;border-bottom:2px solid black;padding:60px 0px 60px 0px;text-align:center;">While the app serves as a critical component to the solution, elements beyond it should also be leveraged as important contributors in order to establish a larger support system.</div>
            
            <div style="clear:both;height:60px;"></div>
            
            <div id="section-subtitle-large" style="margin:0 auto;width:880px;padding:30px 0px 15px 0px;text-align:center;">Defining the solution</div>
            <div id="section-text-middle" style="border:0px solid red;padding:0px 0px 60px 0px;width:600px;font-weight:normal;text-align:justify;">With the key features understood, I began documenting all of the features and details that were needed within the app. This flow extended beyond the app itself and defined the possible methods that could be used to drive users to download the app. All of it was put together in a flow that was intuitive and made sense for the end-user, whether they were the parent or a staff member.</div>
			<div style="margin:0 auto;width:696px;height:409px;background:url(images/laptop-2.png) no-repeat 0px 0px;"></div>
			<div id="section-text-middle" style="border:0px solid red;padding:30px 0px 60px 0px;width:600px;font-weight:normal;text-align:center;">With the blueprint of all the pages and features that needed to be designed, I was ready to proceed to develop the wireframes. If you wish to see this diagram in detail, you can download it here: <a href="pdf/app-architecture-diagram.pdf" target="_blank" style="font-size:16px;" class="link">app architecture diagram</a></div>
        
        </div>
        
        <div style="clear:both;height:10121px;"></div>
        
        <div id="testing-section" style="height:3551px;">
        
        	<div id="section-icon" style="background:url(../images/testing-icon.png) no-repeat 50% 0px;"></div>
            <div id="section-title">Development &amp; refinement</div>
            <div id="section-text-middle" style="width:770px;text-align:center;">This stage helps us distill the design into an optimal solution. Through quick wireframing mockups, prototyping, and extensive testing internally and on the field, everything we do here helps validate the design.</div>
            
            <div style="height:0px;clear:both;"></div>
            
            <div id="section-subtitle-large" style="margin:0 auto;width:880px;padding:0px 0px 15px 0px;border-top:0px solid black;text-align:center;">Quick iterative wireframes</div>
            <div id="section-text-middle" style="border:0px solid red;padding:0px 0px 60px 0px;width:600px;font-weight:normal;text-align:center;">This helped us visualize the design, understand and define interactions, and see how the everything fit together in the app. The best ideas were eventually consolidated into one set that would serve as the blueprint of our app.</div>
            <div style="margin:0 auto;padding:0px 0px 60px 0px;width:880px;height:534px;border-bottom:0px solid black;background:url(images/wireframes.png) no-repeat 0px 0px;"></div>
            <div id="section-text-middle" style="border:0px solid red;padding:0px 0px 60px 0px;width:750px;font-weight:normal;font-size:14px;text-align:center;">If you wish to see this set of wireframes in detail, click on the following link: <a href="pdf/wireframes.pdf" target="_blank" style="font-size:14px;" class="link">download wireframes</a><br /><i><b>*Note that these wireframes primarily represent the sections that are applicable towards a lost child case</b></i></div>
            
            <div style="margin:0 auto;width:880px;height:60px;clear:both;border-top:1px solid black;"></div>
            
            <div id="section-text" style="width:875px;height:489px;background:url(images/testing-hero.png) no-repeat 0 0px;">
            	<div id="section-subtitle-large" style="float:right;width:380px;padding:0px 0px 30px 0px;">Prototyping &amp;<br />internal testing</div>
                <div style="clear:both;"></div>
            	<div id="section-text-middle" style="border:0px solid red;float:right;padding:0px 0px 60px 0px;width:380px;font-weight:normal;text-align:justify;">Developing working prototypes of the app helped validate if elements worked together properly and if things were intuitive and actionable for users. We first tested them internally to resolve the first set of notable issues. This helped smooth everything out before moving on to perform field testing at the park.</div>
            </div>
            <div id="section-subtitle-large" style="margin:0 auto;width:880px;padding:30px 0px 15px 0px;text-align:center;">Field testing</div>
            <div id="section-text-middle" style="border:0px solid red;padding:0px 0px 0px 0px;width:600px;font-weight:normal;text-align:justify;">By taking our internally refined prototype and testing it within the context of the actual environment, real users can experience and test the app to provide more valuable feedback. Common and major issues that arise amongst multiple users will be duly noted and should be resolved while refining the wireframes.</div>
            
            
            <div style="height:0px;clear:both;"></div>
            
            <div style="height:60px;clear:both;"></div>
            <div id="section-subtitle" style="margin:0 auto;width:850px;padding:60px 0px 0px 0px;border-top:2px solid black;text-align:center;">
                Upon realizing the extreme and consistent pain points through our extensive testing, we implemented remedial solutions into our designs. Further testing was performed to ensure these issues were resolved.<br /><br />When the refined designs have been established, we can then move on to packaging everything up in preparation for launch
            </div>
            
        </div>
        
        <div style="clear:both;height:2360px;"></div>
        
        <div id="visuals-section">
        	<div id="section-icon" style="background:url(images/delivery-icon.png) no-repeat 50% 0px;"></div>
            <div id="section-title">Package &amp; delivery</div>
            <div id="section-text-middle" style="width:750px;text-align:center;">After defining a visual and language system for the app, we ensured all the files had the necessary specs and were organized and named properly. We then met with engineers to communicate the designs and handed everything over for them to build it all. Below are the fruits of our labor for this initial build.</div>
            <div style="margin:0 auto;width:880px;">
             	<div style="width:880px;height:790px;background:url(images/visuals-1.png) no-repeat 475px 0px;">
            		<div id="section-subtitle" style="float:rleftt;width:440px;margin:0px 0px 0px 0px;padding:350px 0px 0px 0px;">Brand reflection</div>
                    <div id="section-subtitle-gray" style="clear:both;float:left;width:440px;margin:0px 0px 0px 0px;">The UI was designed to showcase the brand of the theme park</div>
            	</div>
                <div style="margin:-150px 0px 0px 0px;width:880px;height:790px;background:url(images/visuals-2.png) no-repeat 0px 0px;">
            		<div id="section-subtitle" style="float:right;padding:380px 0px 0px 0px;width:440px;">Simple navigation</div>
                    <div id="section-subtitle-gray" style="clear:both;float:right;width:440px;">The navigation provides all the information that a user may need about the park during their visit</div>
            	</div>
                <div style="clear:both;height:60px;"></div>
                <div style="margin:0 auto;width:880px;height:1186px;background:url(images/visuals-3.png) no-repeat 180px 120px;">
            		<div id="section-subtitle" style="float:left;width:300px;margin:0px 0px 0px 0px;padding:150px 0px 0px 0px;">Request assistance</div>
